From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 08:36:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 039/185] segacd: re-read a CDD packet that failed its checksum

The drive is answering now -- hardware returned RS1 = 3, naming the
sub-command it was asked -- but the score read zero, so the answers
were being counted as garbage and discarded.

They were arriving intact. The Genesis can halt this CPU in the middle
of the ten-byte status read, and half a packet from before the halt
joined to half from after fails its checksum however perfect the
drive's transmission was. The registers hold the packet until the next
exchange, so reading them again once the halt is over recovers it.

One retry, in the interrupt, costing microseconds.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 0fe361f..7249963 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -390,6 +390,15 @@ void segacd_int_4_c(void)
 
     cdd_poll();
 
+    /* One re-read if it did not checksum. The Genesis can halt this CPU
+     * in the middle of the ten-byte read, and a packet torn in half
+     * fails its checksum even though the drive sent it perfectly -- so
+     * the answers were arriving and being thrown away. The registers
+     * hold the packet until the next exchange, so simply reading them
+     * again once the halt is over recovers it. */
+    if (!cdd_valid)
+        cdd_poll();
+
     /* Acknowledge -- but only if DTS says there is an outgoing transfer
      * to acknowledge. The BIOS tests that bit first and skips the write
      * when it is clear, and the reason is in what the write does: it
